WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.942
A join is not finished when the SQL runs.

00:00:02.942 --> 00:00:07.012
We still need to validate the result.

00:00:07.012 --> 00:00:10.382
Start with counts. Compare the number of rows before and after the join.

00:00:10.382 --> 00:00:17.937
If the target should contain one row per patient, compare COUNT star with COUNT DISTINCT patient ID.

00:00:17.937 --> 00:00:21.827
If those numbers are very different, investigate why.

00:00:21.827 --> 00:00:24.147
Next, inspect keys.

00:00:24.147 --> 00:00:30.667
Check whether primary keys repeat and whether that repetition is expected at the chosen grain.

00:00:30.667 --> 00:00:35.927
A patient ID repeating in an admission-level result may be correct.

00:00:35.927 --> 00:00:42.772
An admission ID repeating in a result that should contain one row per admission may indicate a problem.

00:00:42.772 --> 00:00:45.657
Then spot-check known examples.

00:00:45.657 --> 00:00:51.677
Choose one entity with multiple related records and one entity with no related records.

00:00:51.677 --> 00:00:56.292
Those cases often reveal preservation and cardinality problems quickly.

00:00:56.292 --> 00:00:59.042
Finally, write a grain sentence.

00:00:59.042 --> 00:01:05.027
For example: one row represents one patient with summarized activity counts.

00:01:05.027 --> 00:01:11.057
If you cannot clearly describe what one row means, the query may still be logically unclear even if

00:01:11.057 --> 00:01:13.032
the syntax is correct.
